WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.289
I analyzed these closed GraphQL
reports to see what works in real life.

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:08.989
And it's incredible what a
variety of bugs I've seen.

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:13.489
Because I have to admit, before I
mostly just tested authorization,

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:17.479
so this was very valuable for me,
and hopefully for you as well.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.259
Enjoy!

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:25.008
In this video we'll go through
The types of bugs to which GraphQL

00:00:25.008 --> 00:00:29.988
led and the most common one, of
course, it was authorization.

00:00:30.428 --> 00:00:34.158
And I also have it split into
three different groups, read,

00:00:34.678 --> 00:00:36.358
create, update, and delete.

00:00:36.618 --> 00:00:38.988
And we'll start with the readbacks.

00:00:39.548 --> 00:00:45.988
And this was by far the biggest group
of them all, with as much as 59 reports.

00:00:46.248 --> 00:00:51.478
Some of them were pretty simple like
this one where you have the query that

00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:57.588
fetches some objects in this type, it's
some media from Instagram, and you don't

00:00:57.588 --> 00:01:01.328
even change the parameter to another
user is not like the typical query.

00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:06.463
either where you change the ID and you get
the data of another user here, you just

00:01:06.563 --> 00:01:09.503
added the email attribute to the request.

00:01:09.843 --> 00:01:16.083
So along these medias, whatever it was,
you would also get emails of users.

00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:21.063
And this is definitely valuable
information, impactful information that

00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:23.223
Instagram does not want to have leaked.

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:29.241
But the majority of reports were pretty
simple ideas like this one and I feel

00:01:29.251 --> 00:01:35.471
like I've seen the same report like
five or six time copy pasted report on

00:01:35.471 --> 00:01:41.001
HackerOne because they had very similar
issues just in different queries.

00:01:41.251 --> 00:01:47.441
You just have some query, you change
the idea, or in this case, the handle,

00:01:47.641 --> 00:01:51.611
and you just get the data of something
you shouldn't be able to access.

00:01:52.241 --> 00:01:56.081
And here, this was 2, 500 bounty.

00:01:56.181 --> 00:01:59.491
There were a lot of reports
that were exactly like this one

00:01:59.811 --> 00:02:01.401
with exactly the same bounty.

00:02:01.881 --> 00:02:05.161
Then there were some that were
a little bit less impactful.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.771
So the bounty was a bit less, but
the reports were very, similar.

00:02:09.451 --> 00:02:14.481
So this is the most typical
GraphQL bug you will ever see.

00:02:15.371 --> 00:02:20.071
With this, we'll jump to the
second biggest group, the create

00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:24.241
or update mutations, and we
had very interesting bugs here.

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:31.141
The most impactful one, I would say was
this one from Watson group by J. Franz

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:33.751
Sibbitt, whatever, you should read this.

00:02:35.551 --> 00:02:41.371
and I understand from the summary
because the report itself is not visible.

00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:45.911
I understand from the summary, the
GraphQL was not supposed to be public.

00:02:45.911 --> 00:02:51.221
So the first step, which is
not visible from, this part is

00:02:51.231 --> 00:02:52.871
the discovery of the endpoint.

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:57.381
Maybe it was, under the different
path, maybe on some hidden subdomain.

00:02:58.231 --> 00:03:04.091
But Then the user could register
on some website, and then as a

00:03:04.091 --> 00:03:08.671
registered user, they could also
have the create admin user mutation.

00:03:08.951 --> 00:03:12.391
So they will go from being
unauthenticated to being authenticated,

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:16.161
but with low privileges, to
being authenticated as the admin.

00:03:16.521 --> 00:03:20.061
So it was reported, it was
rewarded as a critical.

00:03:20.981 --> 00:03:26.761
And I would say it was probably one of
the most impactful bugs of the case study.

00:03:27.781 --> 00:03:28.181
Then.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:34.086
to complete the four operations you can
do with the data because you can read

00:03:34.436 --> 00:03:40.606
create or update but you can also delete
which is always i'm saying you should also

00:03:40.606 --> 00:03:44.586
test for this because it's less natural
to test for deletion it's a little bit

00:03:44.606 --> 00:03:50.733
harder to test for deletion so bugs here
will definitely happen and the one i have

00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:57.106
here from gitlab it is very interesting
what happened here so It's not your

00:03:57.146 --> 00:04:02.506
typical IDO where you just change the ID
and turns out you can't delete the object.

00:04:02.516 --> 00:04:04.016
You shouldn't be able to delete.

00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:09.386
It's the case where you have the
mutation that's called destroy snippet.

00:04:09.726 --> 00:04:14.296
I don't know what snippet is exactly in
GitLab, but we all understand what it is.

00:04:15.006 --> 00:04:18.536
And the title of report is, you
can't delete the repository.

00:04:18.746 --> 00:04:19.726
So how come?

00:04:20.366 --> 00:04:25.056
you can delete the repository
using the delete snippet mutation.

00:04:25.146 --> 00:04:30.406
And the answer is, the hunter
used the identifier like this.

00:04:31.086 --> 00:04:38.066
So they use the destroy snippet
mutation, but then they used this

00:04:38.096 --> 00:04:42.916
identifier, which, and we have the
sort of source code analysis here,

00:04:43.786 --> 00:04:50.726
which caused this argument to not be
a snippet by, but a diff note object.

00:04:51.276 --> 00:04:57.376
And apparently the code that was there
happens to just delete the repository

00:04:57.406 --> 00:05:01.956
when this happened, which does not make
sense at all to me, even by looking at

00:05:01.966 --> 00:05:07.826
the snippets, which seem understandable,
it's hard to accept how this could

00:05:07.836 --> 00:05:10.166
happen, but it's very, interesting.

00:05:10.466 --> 00:05:14.771
And the good information
is this type of identifier.

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.861
This is not something GitLab specific.

00:05:16.911 --> 00:05:20.421
I've seen the same form
of Git in HackerOne.

00:05:20.451 --> 00:05:22.211
I've seen the same in Meta.

00:05:22.831 --> 00:05:25.771
So this is not something
created by GitLab.

00:05:26.021 --> 00:05:29.341
It is more widely used.

00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:35.061
So if you're testing the something
that's that uses GraphQL, you can

00:05:35.151 --> 00:05:41.431
try to do this to confuse what object
are you trying to modify or delete.

00:05:42.547 --> 00:05:46.057
This video is based on the
free part of the case study.

00:05:46.237 --> 00:05:50.537
If you're enjoying it, you'll surely also
like the full article, where there are

00:05:50.537 --> 00:05:55.297
crazy bugs like the either that's only
exploitable for 3 seconds, secondary

00:05:55.297 --> 00:05:59.187
path traversals, race conditions,
cache related issues, and more.

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:00.537
crazy, things.

00:06:00.537 --> 00:06:04.027
And also you will get access to the
whole database with all the reports,

00:06:04.427 --> 00:06:06.957
as well as the other 12 case studies.

00:06:07.137 --> 00:06:10.457
If you want to access them, go
to study at bug bounty explained.

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:11.047
com.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.380
Next we'll go to denial of service bugs.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:20.910
And we had a few reports here,
but only one of them is from, bug

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.410
bounty that was actually rewarded.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:23.570
The other are zero days.

00:06:24.605 --> 00:06:29.515
And if you've watched this presentation
by Ronnie about practical exploitation

00:06:29.515 --> 00:06:35.565
of denial of service in bug bounty, you
know that GraphQL has big, potential to

00:06:35.575 --> 00:06:37.955
be vulnerable to denial of service bugs.

00:06:37.955 --> 00:06:42.585
And I can also confirm this from my
experience that it is quite easy to find.

00:06:43.025 --> 00:06:47.595
The bigger problem is if you can get
reward for it, cause as those bugs

00:06:47.595 --> 00:06:52.390
go, they And they are a bit random
whether the program will reward them

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:56.280
or not, so it's always a bit of a
mystery when you're reporting the bug.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.360
Here they got the bounty of actually
the base bounty was just 1, 000,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:07.650
but then they got the bonus for the
coolest bug of the whole event because

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:12.720
this was the google event So they
got another 5, 000 on top of this.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:14.110
So it was a really nice bug.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.570
They Batched the directives which were
used in a little bit of an unnatural

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.850
way for them So it was very creative
and very nice and nice for google

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:32.760
to reward it as well Then as the
next group, we have SQL injection.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:41.120
And this one, you can, of course,
expect some, to some extent, the SQL

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:46.890
injection in the parameter, because
GraphQL itself, it is not a database.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.760
It is not actually something
that stores your data.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.990
What it does is it takes your HTTP
request with the GraphQL query in it,

00:07:56.375 --> 00:08:00.565
And it translates to something that the
developers implement on the backend.

00:08:01.055 --> 00:08:04.935
So it might be the HTTP request
to some backend service.

00:08:05.055 --> 00:08:10.255
It can just be something that from you
to the server, you send the GraphQL

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:15.115
query and the server makes different
REST API requests on the backend,

00:08:15.115 --> 00:08:19.085
because it's much quicker for the
server than it would be for you to

00:08:19.085 --> 00:08:21.665
send, let's say, 10 REST API requests.

00:08:22.125 --> 00:08:25.635
Another case, maybe SQL query, where.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:32.060
The GraphQL query is transformed to SQL
query and the data is then returned.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.100
So normal SQL injection
is somewhat expected.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.320
But the one that was here in
HackerOne was really, interesting.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.120
Because it actually did not
happen inside of the query.

00:08:44.396 --> 00:08:47.446
it's something that before
the query is executed, sets

00:08:47.456 --> 00:08:49.976
some session keys and values.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:56.826
And apparently it was used internally
without user input ever getting to here.

00:08:56.826 --> 00:08:58.756
So there was no problem with this.

00:08:59.686 --> 00:09:03.116
But then there was some other
code, which I don't think is

00:09:03.116 --> 00:09:09.066
mentioned here, that caused the URL
parameter to land in this session.

00:09:09.076 --> 00:09:10.936
So here you can see the request.

00:09:11.476 --> 00:09:14.496
And you can see there is not even
a GraphQL query in this request.

00:09:14.576 --> 00:09:17.890
There is just the request
to the GraphQL endpoint.

00:09:18.331 --> 00:09:23.851
And you have the query parameter with
the fairly typical Postgres SQL injection

00:09:23.891 --> 00:09:28.381
payload with the sleep comment, and you
can see the times here to confirm it.

00:09:29.231 --> 00:09:31.051
So a very, interesting bug.

00:09:32.111 --> 00:09:34.921
I didn't know we've without
access to the source code.

00:09:35.661 --> 00:09:40.021
someone would thing that you
should also test for SQL injection

00:09:40.031 --> 00:09:45.411
in the parameters of the request
itself, not the GraphQL parameters.

00:09:46.011 --> 00:09:50.171
So it's very interesting and
very, cool bug with, big impact.

00:09:50.211 --> 00:09:55.931
Of course, this was not rewarded because
it's, it was found internally by the

00:09:55.931 --> 00:10:00.121
HackerOne team, but they still submitted
this HackerOne reports and this,

00:10:00.851 --> 00:10:03.571
they, give report the real severity.

00:10:03.681 --> 00:10:06.211
And this was a 10 out of 10 critical.

00:10:06.751 --> 00:10:07.541
Understandably.

00:10:08.946 --> 00:10:13.876
Then we'll go to the next group,
which is not something that I like.

00:10:13.876 --> 00:10:17.836
And if you're a long time viewer of
the channel, you probably know this

00:10:18.606 --> 00:10:23.896
because there were three reports
that I found that were Nothing more

00:10:23.896 --> 00:10:30.746
but reporting just the fact that the
GraphQL schema was disclosed, which by

00:10:30.746 --> 00:10:35.156
itself is not a vulnerability, it can
allow vulnerabilities to happen, and

00:10:35.156 --> 00:10:37.196
it can make the exploitation easier.

00:10:37.346 --> 00:10:40.986
But by itself, I don't think
there's any security impact.

00:10:41.826 --> 00:10:44.996
But some people did report it.

00:10:45.046 --> 00:10:49.896
And this report that I have here,
It even got the bounty of, I

00:10:49.896 --> 00:10:53.226
think he said between six and 800.

00:10:53.226 --> 00:10:59.536
So you can probably try to do
it, but I would not advise it.

00:10:59.546 --> 00:11:04.866
If you have so much data, try to find
what queries and mutations you can use

00:11:05.546 --> 00:11:09.746
or do what we did or the hundred it
in the beginning of the video, where.

00:11:10.096 --> 00:11:13.956
You have the usual query, which is
something you have access to it.

00:11:13.956 --> 00:11:18.426
It's not a violation of any access
control, but you just add some

00:11:18.456 --> 00:11:22.976
attribute to it that all of a sudden
leaks the sensitive data back to you.

00:11:22.996 --> 00:11:27.296
This is what I think you should
try if you have the introspection.

00:11:28.286 --> 00:11:33.886
And finally, we have one last
group, the CSRF, and I very much

00:11:33.886 --> 00:11:36.696
liked the finding that was here.

00:11:36.716 --> 00:11:39.886
It is actually And not the
bug bounty, it is the security

00:11:39.936 --> 00:11:41.576
auditing report by Doyensec.

00:11:42.706 --> 00:11:46.936
And they found the CSRF
using the head method.

00:11:47.926 --> 00:11:54.026
And if you remember a few years
ago, there was the OCSRF in GitHub

00:11:54.336 --> 00:11:56.376
that used exactly the same method.

00:11:56.376 --> 00:12:03.276
So there was code like this, which sort of
explicitly checked if the method is get.

00:12:03.416 --> 00:12:07.976
And then there was some things that
were happening if the method was get,

00:12:08.096 --> 00:12:13.306
were not state changing because the
get is not subject to CSRF protection.

00:12:13.796 --> 00:12:16.726
So it cannot change or modify any data.

00:12:17.486 --> 00:12:22.816
So in context of GraphQL speaking
directly, it should, get should

00:12:22.936 --> 00:12:26.406
only be allowed to do GraphQL
queries and not GraphQL mutations.

00:12:27.421 --> 00:12:31.311
But here there was the explicit
check for the get method.

00:12:31.781 --> 00:12:37.131
And the thing is, automatically
the router would send head requests

00:12:37.131 --> 00:12:39.251
to the same sort of piece of code.

00:12:39.321 --> 00:12:45.091
So heads would implicitly land
in the same function as gets.

00:12:45.691 --> 00:12:50.081
But then when it comes to the
check like this, head is no longer

00:12:50.081 --> 00:12:52.271
treated the same way as the get is.

00:12:52.811 --> 00:13:00.341
So head would Not have the CSR protection
and then not get to this non state

00:13:00.341 --> 00:13:02.021
changing part of the source code.

00:13:02.091 --> 00:13:04.961
So it would land in the
same place as post would.

00:13:05.581 --> 00:13:10.741
And you could easily just have
the query in the URL parameter.

00:13:11.251 --> 00:13:12.761
So a very, nice report.

00:13:12.791 --> 00:13:15.271
I really like the one in
GitHub a few years ago.

00:13:15.781 --> 00:13:18.351
And I really like this one as well.

00:13:19.461 --> 00:13:23.351
And this is everything when it comes
to, the back classes in GraphQL.

